course: dictionary
lesson: 10923
title: What is voltage?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Voltage is the push that makes electric current flow around a circuit. Think of it like the push that moves water along a pipe: the stronger the push, the more the water rushes along. Voltage is measured in volts, written with the letter V.
explain: «A battery marked 1.5 V has a voltage of 1.5 volts.» The V tells you how big the push is. When you join more cells in a line, the voltage goes up, and the bulb gets brighter because the push is bigger.
explain: Voltage can also be dangerous. «Signs say "Danger: High Voltage".» Mains electricity has a high voltage, so you must never touch sockets or wires with wet hands.
fact: Voltage is the push that makes electric current flow around a circuit.
fact: Voltage is measured in volts, shown with the letter V.
fact: A higher voltage gives a bigger push.
fact: «Pylons are tall steel towers that hold high-voltage wires high above the ground.»
fact: «Electric eels can give shocks of several hundred volts, about 600 or even more.»
exercise: What is voltage? | The name of a light bulb | *The push that makes electric current flow around a circuit | The amount of water in a bath | The colour of a wire
exercise: What unit is voltage measured in? | Litres | Kilograms | Metres | *Volts
exercise: «A battery marked 1.5 V has a voltage of 1.5 volts.» When more cells are joined in a line, why does the bulb get brighter? | Because the battery gets colder | *Because the voltage goes up, giving a bigger push | Because the wires get shorter | Because the bulb gets heavier
test: Which gives a bigger push, a low voltage or a high voltage? | *High voltage | Low voltage | They push the same | Neither pushes
test: Why should you never touch sockets or wires with wet hands? | Because wet hands make the wires heavier | *Because mains electricity has a high voltage and is dangerous | Because the wires are cold | Because sockets do not like water
exam: «Electricity travels long distances at very high voltage because less is wasted that way.» Why is electricity sent at very high voltage over long distances? | Because pylons need a fresh coat of paint | *Because less electricity is wasted | Because it makes the wires smell nice | Because it makes the lights go out
